🧠 Definitions

Assume a sorted array arr

🔹 lower_bound(key)
first index i such that
    arr[i] >= key
🔹 upper_bound(key)
first index i such that
    arr[i] > key

🎯 Example
arr = [1, 3, 3, 3, 8]

For key = 3
| Function       | Result index | Value        |
| -------------- | ------------ | ------------ |
| lower_bound(3) | 1            | first 3      |
| upper_bound(3) | 4            | first >3 → 8 |

📊 Visual
index:  0  1  2  3  4
arr:    1  3  3  3  8
             ^
          lower_bound(3)

                    ^
                upper_bound(3)

🔧 Java Implementation (Manual)
================================
int lowerBound(int[] arr, int key) {
    int l = 0, r = arr.length - 1;
    while (l < r) {
        int m = l + (r - l) / 2;
        if (arr[m] >= key) {
            r = m;
        } else {
            l = m + 1;
        }
    }
    return l;
}

============================
int upperBound(int[] arr, int key) {
    int l = 0, r = arr.length;
    while (l < r) {
        int m = l + (r - l) / 2;
        if (arr[m] > key) {
            r = m;
        } else {
            l = m + 1;
        }
    }
    return l;
}

==================
Notice the only change:

>=  vs  >
================

🔍 Using Arrays.binarySearch for both
=====================
//Lower Bound
int idx = Arrays.binarySearch(arr, key);
if (idx < 0) idx = -idx - 1;
=====================
upper_bound trick
To simulate upper_bound, we search for:
key + ε
Since we have integers, we do:
===============
int idx = Arrays.binarySearch(arr, key + 1);
if (idx < 0) idx = -idx - 1;
=============
🎯 In your weighted random problem

You specifically need:

lower_bound(prefix, randomVal)


because we want:

first prefix >= randomVal


NOT upper bound.

🧠 Mental model

Think in terms of ranges:

lower_bound(key) → start of range
upper_bound(key) → end of range

🏁 Interview cheat line

“lower_bound finds first element ≥ key, upper_bound finds first element > key. They differ only in one comparison sign.”